[{:seon.task/name "seon-source-is-the-program"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: the load effect reads Seon's source (`:seon.config.source/dirs` under `:seon.config.source/root`) with Jolt's reader (exact text, positions, file order) and the analyzer (analysis only), so seon.* and my.* namespaces have form, var and ref facts on main and agents change platform functions on their branches like any program code (docs/source.md#import-the-running-generations-source-as-mains-program). The source is config (`:seon.config.source/root`, absent: no import and no write-back; `dirs`, `branch`), read in `seon.root/start!`; every `{:seon.db/edn true}` config key is stored as EDN text by its schema (`seon.config/facts`, `seon.config/from-db`). The binary is the running generation; main's facts are the next generation's source."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/importing-src-records-every-form-exactly}}
 {:seon.task/name "merged-forms-write-back-exactly"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: a file effect writes main's merged program back to its files (`seon.source/file-text`, `seon.fx/write-file`): each namespace's file is its ns form and forms in recorded order, exact text, untouched forms byte-identical (docs/source.md#write-back-mains-files-byte-for-byte). Fidelity proof: import, write back with no merge, zero diff, on a copy of the tree, never the live tree."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.source/seon-source-is-the-program"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/import-then-write-back-is-a-zero-diff
                      seon.source-test/a-merged-change-rewrites-only-its-form}}
 {:seon.task/name "merges-are-committed-and-run-next"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.fx/git-commit` commits written files on branch `jolt/seon-merges` of the source tree (never main, never pushed), its message naming the merged tasks and runs from facts; the asker records the sha (docs/source.md#commit-the-merges-branch). Seon is author and committer (`:seon.config.source/committer-name`, `committer-email`): a root's environment and the proof's tree have no git identity, so the proof fails if the identity is dropped. bin/check loop commits on a clone."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.source/merged-forms-write-back-exactly"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-merge-is-committed-on-the-merges-branch}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-writes-back-only-what-it-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "A form present in the source root but absent from the running binary is lost by a merge's write-back. Reproduction: bin/check 100's e2e-loop with target/seon built from main e117b9b and the source tree at a branch adding the private fn `seon.db/stored-id`: the loop's merge commit changed src/seon/fixtures.clj as asked and also deleted `stored-id` from src/seon/db.clj (12 lines), LOOP FAILED; on a binary rebuilt from the same source the loop passes (:files src/seon/fixtures.clj only). A binary older than its source is the normal case after a merge loads in place (docs/source.md#a-merge-loads-in-place), so this can delete merged code. Cause not found: the merge's delta, or the write-back's rendering of the file, treats the form as retracted. Correct: the write-back renders exactly the files whose forms the merge changed, from main's live forms, whatever the binary holds. The proof is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/a-merge-on-an-older-binary-keeps-the-forms-it-lacks}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-import-reads-a-changed-ns-form-before-its-forms"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.source/analyze-form` always runs a file's ns form first, as loading the file would (running it again on the namespace it set up changes nothing), so a merged require does not stop the next boot on a binary older than its source. Reproduction before: a binary built before src/my/schema.clj's ns form added `[seon.config :as config]` died at boot in `seon.root/import-source!` -> `seon.source/replace-tx` -> ... -> `seon.source/analyze-form` with `No such namespace: config`; the standing root at main 6848851 stopped the same way (`No such namespace: ts`). Cause: at boot `seon.program/load-all!` runs main's old ns forms, and an old ns form's require of another test namespace marked that one loaded, so analyze-form (which ran ns forms only for namespaces not in loaded-libs) skipped its new ns form."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-changed-ns-form-applies-its-requires-before-its-forms}}
 {:seon.task/name "test-namespaces-built-into-the-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "A root importing test/ restarts in 8.7-9.1 s against 3.5-3.6 s without it, and holds 119-130 MB against 111 MB (`seon.lib/real-mb`; 382 namespaces against 333), measured with bin/root start at main 9382656; fresh, the import itself is 4.5 s for 131 files and 1,923 forms against 2.9 s for 80 and 1,288. Cause: the binary (bin/build seon: `jolt build -m seon.check`) compiles src only, so `seon.program/load-all!` evaluates every test form one by one at each boot. The owner's standing root imports test/ (the main tree's config/local.edn) and pays it on every restart; `:seon.config.source/dirs` leaves test/ out for this reason (resources/seon/config/default.edn). Correct: the binary holds the test namespaces as it holds src's, so load-all! finds them held and loads none, and a root importing test/ boots in about the src-only time; then the dirs can default to the standing root's. Check first what v1 did and whether Jolt's build takes a second source root (jolt build's :paths, a deps alias :test) without the test runner's main. Proof (not written): a restart of a root importing src and test/ costs no more than 1.2x a src-only restart, measured with `seon.profile/now-ns`. Step E's dry run (2026-10-22) adds why it matters: a worktree root on the default dirs cannot work any task whose proof belongs in an existing test namespace (the design agent could not load seon.e2e-test or seon.test-store), so every worktree that runs Seon agents on Seon must add test/ as the owner's root does."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-root-importing-test-restarts-as-fast-as-src}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-trees-commit-merges-three-way"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: when main's merged forms and a worktree session's edits to the same function meet at import, the tree wins: `seon.source/replace-tx` makes main's program the tree's, asked by `seon.root/import-source!` (docs/source.md). So a form an agent merged since the tree's commit is lost when the tree, unchanged there, is imported. Correct: the import is the tree's program merged into main by `seon.merge/three-way` against their merge base (main's program at the tree commit it last imported or wrote back), so only what the tree changed applies, and a both-sides change is an unmerged entry like any merge's. Deletes replace-tx's whole-program overwrite."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/an-import-keeps-a-form-main-merged-since-the-trees-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "boot-imports-before-it-loads"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: at boot `seon.root/start!` runs `seon.program/load-all!` before the import (`seon.root/import-source!`), so a namespace the binary does not build (test/'s) runs its old forms in root and the fork server until the next boot (docs/source.md). Correct: boot imports the tree first and load-all! then loads main's forms once, so what runs is what main holds after the import. Read first what the import's analysis needs loaded. Task seon.source/test-namespaces-built-into-the-binary removes the case for test/; this one is for any namespace the binary lacks."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-form-changed-in-the-tree-runs-at-the-first-boot}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-import-reads-only-changed-files"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: every bin/test run, and a root importing its tree, reads and analyzes every file (`seon.fx/read-source`, `seon.source/accounts`), about 8 s per run (docs/source.md#tests-run-by-reach). Correct: results reused by input digest (AGENTS §5): main records each file's digest beside :ns/file, and the import reads and analyzes only the files whose digest differs, so an unchanged tree imports nothing. Done (worktree session pure-tests, 2026-10-23): `seon.source/refresh-tx` brings a program up to date with the files that changed (a removed one's text nil). It reads their namespaces again, and a namespace that requires one only when that one's interface changed (`seon.source/interface`: names and kinds, macros, protocols, types, inline functions, the ns form). Each form is written only where it differs from db (`seon.program/account-diff-tx`, no temporary db), so the program is the same as replace-tx of the whole tree, which is that same diff over every file. On Seon's src+test, a body edit in a 29-form leaf went from 151+25 ms to 33+3 ms (read and analysis 20, interface gate 2, diff and tx 9, transact 3), and one in seon.edn (21 dependents) costs 2 ms. Each phase is flat in program size. Its caller names the changed files: the test service's watcher (health, the tests branch) does. Left: root's boot import records each file's digest beside :ns/file and calls refresh-tx with the files whose digest differs."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-refresh-is-the-whole-trees-replace-at-the-cost-of-what-changed seon.source-test/a-body-edit-writes-only-its-form seon.source-test/a-fixed-change-costs-the-same-at-any-program-size}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-test-contract-compiles-where-it-loads"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen by run #11's investigation (2026-10-23): the fork server's load of test/ on next-writeback-order's root answered `:load-errors [{:ns seon.source-test :message \"seon.source-test/diff-of: its contract does not compile...\"}]`, though the test runner loads the same namespace green. A test helper's contract must compile wherever main's program loads (root, the fork server, an agent). Find what the fork server's registry lacks for `[:tuple :seon.host/db :map :map :set]` and fix it at its owner. Proof: the fork server's load of test/ has no load errors."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "another-trees-ns-form-analyzes-beside-the-image"
  :seon.task/plan "Found on jolt/docs-any-md, 2026-11-01: bin/check health (test-runner/health!) analyzes the base commit's program in the image of the branch's (program-db of the base tree, test-runner/keep-tree!, seon.source/refresh-tx), and seon.source/analyze-form runs each ns form in the live namespace. A branch that points an alias at another namespace (seon.problems' [seon.docs :as docs] became [seon.doc :as docs]) made the base's ns form throw IllegalStateException \"Alias docs already exists in namespace seon.problems, aliasing seon.doc\" (Jolt's ns-alias-check!, reference-code/jolt/host/chez/ns.ss), and the whole health step failed. Clearing the namespace's aliases before its ns form (which analyze-form's docstring already promises: the file's aliases, whatever the namespace held) only moved it: the base's ns form requires namespaces that load from the checkout's disk (my.test, new code), which then clash the other way. Worked around by aliasing seon.doc as doc, so no alias name changes its target. The cause: one image analyzes two programs whose namespaces disagree. Done 2026-11-01 (jolt/refresh-skips-removed-dependents, for the context lab's parent-tree dbs): refresh-tx never reads a removed dependent's old text again; a namespace whose file the checkout lacks is marked loaded once analyzed (seon.source/in-checkout?) and read again from db's text with a change; an ns form's alias pointing elsewhere is cleared before it runs (only that alias). Proof seon.source-test/a-renamed-alias-target-analyzes-beside-the-image covers the removed dependent, the off-checkout require both ways and the repointed alias; the re-read of an unchanged off-checkout namespace is shown only by the lab's runner on Seon's pre-rename trees (an in-process test could not reproduce a process that never held it). Left, the cleaner cause: an analysis of another tree that never loads a namespace from disk (its requires answered by the tree's own files, as the analysis reads them, or each tree analyzed in a process of its own), and the proof seon.source-test/a-renamed-alias-target-analyzes-beside-the-image: a base tree aliasing x as a.old and a head aliasing x as a.new both analyze in one process."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-renamed-alias-target-analyzes-beside-the-image}}
 {:seon.task/name "seon-reader-is-the-one-program-reader"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: program text is read by clojure.core/read under a bound *ns* in some places and by seon.reader in others, so ::alias/kw resolution depends on the call site (ece53cd25f moved one site). Still on clojure.core/read: seon.source/read-forms (used by accounts and by the defs map), ns-form, and two sites in seon.repl; seon.config/parse-set and seon.reader swallow the read error and answer a generic message. The change: seon.reader is the one reader of program text; those sites use it; a read error is the reader's own, named; a bin/laws rule lists clojure.core/read and read-string in src as catch is listed. Docs: docs/source.md and docs/failure-points.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/an-unloaded-namespaces-alias-keyword-reads}}]
